      "id": "245d81138faf00f3056fdef7569990dbcb02c18b19f9c2933faff429fb7ec0e7",
      "title": "682: quantum of sollazzo",
      "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T22:14:10.246Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "Economic and fiscal outlook – October 2026 - GOV.UK",
      "pageDescription": "",
      "evidenceText": "Economic and fiscal outlook – October 2026 - GOV.UK { \"@context\": \"http://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntityOfPage\": { \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"@id\": \"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026\" }, \"name\": \"Economic and fiscal outlook – October 2026\", \"datePublished\": \"2026-10-30T16:13:15+00:00\", \"dateModified\": \"2026-10-30T14:21:10+00:00\", \"text\": null, \"publisher\": { \"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"GOV.UK\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk\", \"logo\": { \"@type\": \"ImageObject\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk/assets/frontend/govuk_publishing_components/govuk-logo-b8553f688131fad665e52a8c2df7633f9cd1c0fffb9f69703cc68c728e7b3b74.png\" } }, \"image\": [ \"https://www.gov.uk/assets/frontend/govuk_publishing_components/govuk-schema-placeholder-1x1-2672c0fb7a5d5f947d880522c509ebe7f2be090885883cc94418f6860e812e15.png\", \"https://www.gov.uk/assets/frontend/govuk_publishing_components/govuk-schema-placeholder-4x3-194fde4197f00e669f6f52c182df2ed707bfb2024c9ef39f7a2ed20da62b90eb.png\", \"https://www.gov.uk/assets/frontend/govuk_publishing_components/govuk-schema-placeholder-16x9-30e6c0e035636ee6b9dc72ae254bcd4a925182805afe7c5b7170cf2394894b28.png\" ], \"author\": { \"@type\": \"Organization\", \"name\": \"Office for Budget Responsibility\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk/government/organisations/office-for-budget-responsibility\" }, \"mainEntity\": [ { \"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Foreword\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026#foreword\", \"acceptedAnswer\": { \"@type\": \"Answer\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026#foreword\", \"text\": \"\\u003cp\\u003eThis Economic and fiscal outlook (EFO) sets out our central forecast, alongside the risks and uncertainties that surround it, for the five years to 2031-32. It takes account of recent data and other developments, alongside government policies announced since our last forecast – up to and including the October 2026 Budget. The forecasts presented in this document represent our view as the three independent members of the OBR’s Budget Responsibility Committee (BRC). We take full responsibility for the judgements that underpin them and for the conclusions we have reached.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAs always, we have been greatly supported in our work by the staff of the OBR. We are very grateful for their hard work and expertise. We have also drawn on the work and expertise of officials across government in preparing these forecasts. We are grateful for their engagement and insight.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe present the economy forecast in calendar years and produce quarterly paths. The fiscal forecast is typically presented on a financial year basis which runs from April to March. For the current fiscal year, 2026-27, outturn data is incomplete and is, therefore, presented as an OBR forecast. Our online detailed forecast tables, and charts and tables data files, detail the points where individual forecast variables are outturn and forecasts.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOn 20 July, we provided an assessment of the outlook for the economy and public finances to the incoming Chancellor of the Exchequer, to support his initial preparations ahead of the Budget. This assessment was subsequently used for the initial round of our pre-measures forecast. The date for the Budget was then announced on 31 July, which provided more than the ten weeks’ notice required by the Memorandum of understanding between the Office for Budget Responsibility, HM Treasury, the Department for Work and Pensions and HM Revenue and Customs (MoU). After this, the timetable for the production of the forecast proceeded as follows:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eOver the remainder of the summer, the OBR developed the pre-measures economic forecast, drawing on new data releases and other information available during this period. At various points, using the economic determinants from these forecasts (such as the components of nominal income and spending, unemployment, inflation, and interest rates), we commissioned updated forecasts from the relevant government departments for the various tax and spending items that in aggregate determine the position of the public finances. We discussed these in detail with the officials producing them, which allowed us to investigate proposed changes in forecasting methodology and to assess the significance of recent tax and spending outturn data.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eThe pre-measures economy forecast was completed on 8 September and was based on financial and energy market data averaged over the 10 working days to 19 August. This is a longer gap between the closure of the pre-measures economy forecast and publication of the final forecast than is set out within the MoU, and was requested by the Treasury to allow for greater policy decision-making time after receiving the final pre-measures forecast. We agreed to this change on the basis that it would also provide the OBR with greater time to effectively scrutinise the economic and fiscal impact of policy decisions, and to finalise and quality assure our analysis. We will review the effectiveness of this change with the Treasury ahead of our next forecast.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eOnce completed, the pre-measures economy forecast was sent to government departments to produce individual tax and spending forecasts and they returned them on the same day. These fed into a final pre-measures fiscal forecast that was completed on 18 September. The fiscal forecast is consistent with the ONS Public sector finances (PSF) release on 21 August, including ONS estimates of revisions that would arise in the 22 September release, as well as management information on tax receipts for August. In line with recent practice, we took a later window for interest rates in the fiscal forecast, over the five working days to 15 September. This results in very minor discrepancies between the economy and fiscal forecast which we judge to be immaterial.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eThe final pre-measures economic and fiscal forecast was sent to the Chancellor on 18 September. At the request of the Treasury, this was the only pre-measures economic and fiscal forecast round that was shared with them during this forecast. This represented a change from the process followed in recent years, when we shared interim rounds of the pre-measures economic and fiscal forecasts with the Treasury as they were completed. No further material adjustments were made to our economy or fiscal forecasts after this, other than to take account of the impact of policy measures provided by the Treasury. We always reserve the right to reopen our pre-measures forecasts in the event of exceptional circumstances which lead to a significant and structural change in the economic and fiscal outlook. We did not judge that to be the case at this event.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eIn parallel to the production of the forecast, we scrutinised the costing of a number of individual tax and spending measures announced since the March 2026 forecast. As usual, OBR staff and the BRC requested further information and/or changes to many of the draft costings prepared by HMRC, DWP and other departments.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eWe made an initial assessment of the economic and fiscal effects of the emerging Budget policy package that was provided by the Treasury on 25 September. We incorporated this package into an initial post-measures forecast, to provide an early view on the effect of policy measures on the economy and public finances, which we sent to the Chancellor on 2 October. This forecast round was produced using our internal ready-reckoner models rather than being sent to departmental forecasters.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n \\u003cli\\u003e\\n \\u003cp\\u003eIn line with the agreed timetable, on 9 October the Treasury provided the final package of measures that could cause movements in our economy forecast. We sent the resulting final economy forecast to the Treasury on 16 October and a near-final fiscal forecast on 20 October. Final policy decisions, that would only affect the fiscal forecast, were returned by the Treasury on 20 October, and our forecast was then finalised and sent to the Treasury on 22 October.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n \\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe Treasury made a written request, as provided for in the MoU between us, that we provide the Chancellor and an agreed list of special advisers and officials with a near-final draft of the EFO on 22 October. This allowed the Treasury to prepare the Chancellor’s statement and accompanying documents. We also provided pre-release access to the full and final EFO on 26 October.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe agreed forecast timetable was largely adhered to. Small changes were made to the parameters of one policy measure following the costings certification deadline, meaning that, although we have no concerns about the methodology used, we have not been able to certify this costing. We have used the Government’s estimates of its fiscal impact and will return to it at the next forecast. In addition, two policy measures with direct impacts on our economy forecast were altered after it had been finalised. Had we been able to incorporate these, they would have only made a small difference to our final economy forecast.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eDuring the forecasting period, the BRC held dozens of scrutiny and challenge meetings with officials from other departments, in addition to numerous further meetings at staff level and with external stakeholders. We have been provided with all the information and analysis that we requested and have come under no pressure from Ministers, advisers, or officials to change any of our conclusions. A full log of our substantive contact with Ministers, their offices, and special advisers since we published our previous forecast in March 2026 can be found on our website. This includes the list of special advisers and officials who received the near-final draft of the EFO on 22 October.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIn this EFO, we present a detailed central forecast in line with the responsibility in our primary legislation to assess the extent to which the fiscal mandate has been, or is likely to be, achieved. The fiscal mandate is currently defined as a target in the third year of our forecast. This requires us to produce a precise forecast for a set of very uncertain outcomes. For example, the borrowing forecast represents the difference between over £1.5 trillion each of receipts and expenditure by the end of the forecast period. We therefore also present a range of different indicators of uncertainty to illustrate the potential effects of alternative scenarios, sensitivities and risks that inevitably lie around the central forecast. For fiscal policy-makers, and those who scrutinise fiscal policy, we see this analysis as being as important as the central forecast. The central forecast remains our best estimate of the central outlook for the economy and public finances, within the constraints in which we are required to make it.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe would be pleased to receive feedback on any aspect of the content or presentation of our analysis. This can be sent to feedback@obr.uk.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eThe Budget Responsibility Committee\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\\nProfessor Jonathan Haskel CBE, Professor David Miles CBE and Tom Josephs\\u003c/p\\u003e\" } }, { \"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Chapter 1: Executive summary\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026#chapter-1-executive-summary\", \"acceptedAnswer\": { \"@type\": \"Answer\", \"url\": \"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/october-2026-economic-and-fiscal-outlook/economic-and-fiscal-outlook-october-2026#chapter-1-executive-summary\", \"text\": \"\\u003ch3 id=\\\"overview\\\"\\u003eOverview\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.1\\u003c/strong\\u003e The UK’s economic and fiscal position remains very challenging. Since 2008, repeated adverse shocks have contributed to persistently weak GDP growth and elevated levels of government borrowing. This has resulted in a sharp increase in government debt as a share of GDP, to a level that is high by both recent historical and international standards.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.2\\u003c/strong\\u003e There have been further adverse developments since our March forecast, with both interest rates and energy prices rising significantly. As a net importer of energy, the rise in energy prices constitutes a negative terms-of-trade shock for the UK, which pushes up on inflation and lowers real household disposable incomes. In the central forecast – the mid-point of a wide range of possible outcomes – CPI inflation is 3½ per cent in 2027, compared to 2 per cent expected in March. Real GDP growth slows from around 1¼ per cent in 2026 to 1 per cent in 2027 – the latter around a ½ percentage point lower than the March forecast. Assuming energy prices subsequently decline in line with market pricing, inflation returns to 2 per cent by 2028 and GDP returns to the level expected in March by 2030. After falling slightly in the near term, per person real household disposable income in 2030-31 is over ¾ per cent below our March forecast and only 2 per cent above its pre-pandemic level.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.3\\u003c/strong\\u003e The rise in interest rates and increase in the inflation forecast leaves pre-measures borrowing an average of £11 billion per year higher than in March. The direct effects of policy measures further increase borrowing by £8 billion in 2026-27, £4 billion in 2027-28, and £0.4 billion in 2028-29, and then reduce borrowing by £7 billion, on average, in the years thereafter. Overall, while borrowing as a share of GDP is still forecast to decline over the forecast to just under 2 per cent of GDP, these changes slow the near-term pace of consolidation compared to previous plans. Public sector net debt peaks around 97 per cent of GDP in 2028-29, before falling slightly to 96 per cent in 2031-32. In the central forecast, the fiscal mandate for the current budget to be in surplus in 2029-30 is met by a margin of £15 billion. The supplementary target for public sector net financial liabilities (PSNFL) to be falling as a share of GDP in 2029-30 is met by a margin of £24 billion.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.4\\u003c/strong\\u003e The outlook for the economy and public finances is uncertain, with significantly better or worse outcomes of broadly equal probability. If energy prices reach a higher and more sustained peak, borrowing could be £17 billion above the central forecast in 2027-28, while a lower energy price path could reduce borrowing by £12 billion. A one per cent change in interest rates could raise or reduce government debt interest costs by £16 billion. If productivity growth were 0.5 percentage points higher or lower than forecast, borrowing could reach £50 billion lower or £40 billion higher. Significant pressures on departmental spending include higher inflation, public sector pay, and policy ambitions such as defence.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"economic-and-fiscal-context\\\"\\u003eEconomic and fiscal context\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.5 Adverse shocks have continued to hit the UK since the 2008 global financial crisis\\u003c/strong\\u003e, \\u003cstrong\\u003eincluding rising barriers to trade after EU exit, the pandemic, and the 2022 energy price spike\\u003c/strong\\u003e. These shocks, alongside other structural trends, have resulted in weak growth in GDP per person, periods of elevated inflation, and low growth in real household disposable incomes. On top of these, since we finalised our March forecast, there has been another spike in energy prices and a significant rise in interest rates. Gilt yields have returned closer to their long-run averages following a period of exceptionally low rates after the financial crisis.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.6 These adverse shocks and low economic growth have resulted in elevated levels of government borrowing and debt in the UK\\u003c/strong\\u003e. Chart 1.1 shows that UK borrowing has followed a similar trend to the G7 and advanced economy average over the past two decades but at a consistently higher level. As a result, UK government debt as a share of GDP has increased by more than in any other G7 economy, rising from 38 per cent in 2006 to reach 93 per cent in 2026. The cost of servicing this debt has also increased sharply recently – from 1.7 per cent of GDP (£39 billion) in 2019-20 to 3.5 per cent of GDP (£109 billion) in 2025-26. In the IMF’s forecasts, UK borrowing falls to close to the advanced economy average by 2031, which would lead to debt stabilising at around the G7 average.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch4 id=\\\"chart-11-general-government-borrowing-and-net-debt-in-advanced-economies\\\"\\u003eChart 1.1: General government borrowing and net debt in advanced economies\\u003c/h4\\u003e\\u003cfigure class=\\\"image embedded\\\"\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"img\\\"\\u003e\\u003cimg src=\\\"https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae4c1453d3bf10d23546245/chart-1.1.svg\\\" alt=\\\"\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003c/figure\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eNote: This chart shows IMF outturn (2006-2025) and forecast (2026-2031) for general government net borrowing and net debt which excludes the activity of public corporations. The lines show borrowing and debt for the UK, the unweighted G7 average excluding the UK and the unweighted advanced economy average excluding the UK. The advanced economies included in the average are those detailed in the IMF World economic outlook. The swathe shows the range of borrowing and debt for G7 economies excluding the UK.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSource: IMF, OBR\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"economic-outlook\\\"\\u003eEconomic outlook\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e1.7 Energy prices have increased sharply since our last forecast in March\\u003c/strong\\u003e. The left panel of Chart 1.2 shows that market pricing, at the time that our forecast was conditioned, implied that oil prices would fall from their peak of 95 dollars a barrel in the second quarter of this year to just above 70 dollars a barrel by late 2028 and remain around that level thereafter. The right panel shows that gas prices were expected to peak at around 150 pence a therm at the end of this year before falling back to around 70 pence a therm from mid-2028. Compared with the March forecast, market pricing for oil and gas is significantly higher in the near term, by 30 and 80 per cent respectively, but in our central forecast they are only around 10 per cent higher in 2030. Oil and gas futures have increased further since we took market expectations – at the time of finalising this report, they were close to the top of the range since the beginning of the Middle East conflict. Given the uncertainty around the outlook for energy prices, we have produced scenarios assessing the potential economic and fiscal implications of alternative paths for these in Chapter 7.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch4 id=\\\"chart-12-energy-prices\\\"\\u003eChart 1.2: Energy prices\\u003c/h4\\u003e\\u003cfigure class=\\\"image embedded\\\"\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"img\\\"\\u003e\\u003cimg src=\\\"https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae4c145ec36e258a19984b4/chart-1.2.svg\\\" alt=\\\"\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003c/figure\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eNote: October 2026 forecast uses ",
