      "pageDescription": "Finland tops the World Happiness Report, yet an experimental smile index puts it 114th out of 125. What do the rankings actually measure?",
      "evidenceText": "Don't Lie to Us, Finland: The Happiness Report Has a Problem Press ESC to close Or check our Popular Categories... Health & Medicine Nutrition Longevity Supplements Exercise & Performance Mind & Well-being Want to know how happy someone is? Ask them. Or watch how often they smile. But what if those two approaches give you completely different answers? We chose the second approach and gathered observations of smiling from dozens of research sources. The ranking we produced bore little resemblance to the one in the famous World Happiness Report. In the television series Lie to Me , Dr. Cal Lightman spotted discrepancies between what people said and what their faces showed. Lightman is fictional, but the character was inspired by psychologist Paul Ekman [1] , one of the best-known researchers of emotional expressions. We decided to try a similar approach on entire countries rather than individual people. Finland ranks first in the World Happiness Report 2026. Our experimental model of observed smiling placed it 114th out of 125 countries and territories. Egypt came first, although its position rests on just two groups of research sources. That does not necessarily mean Egyptians are happier than Finns. So why did the two rankings produce such different answers? We began with an axiom: how often people smile spontaneously is the main directly observable sign of happiness as they experience it. Testing that idea across countries would require comparable observations, and there are very few. We decided to see what the existing research on smiling could tell us anyway. Can a smile reveal happiness? Ekman’s research In 1990, Paul Ekman, Richard Davidson and Wallace Friesen studied people’s facial expressions as they watched emotionally charged videos. They focused on the Duchenne smile, which engages muscles around the eyes as well as the mouth. The longer such a smile lasted, the happier participants rated themselves (correlation r = 0.59 ). Its association with amusement was stronger still ( r = 0.70 ). Watch people for long enough in comparable situations, and how often they produce certain kinds of smiles may reveal something about their emotional experience. But a smile is no foolproof test of sincerity: people can produce one deliberately, and a happy person need not smile all the time. Gardiner and colleagues [2] later found more modest associations between smiling frequency and overall self-rated happiness (r = 0.18 and 0.26 in two contexts). Happiness on camera: 405,000 video recordings In December 2025, researchers at Google Research asked a simple question: why rely on surveys alone when we can observe people smiling? They analyzed 405,448 short video recordings from 233 volunteers . A smartphone camera recorded each person’s face after the screen was unlocked, while they used the phone as usual. A neural network measured how intensely they smiled. The smiles were weakest on Mondays and strongest on Sundays. That weekly rhythm closely matched changes in happiness reported by an independent US survey. The correlation was r = 0.92. Two very different measures, facial expressions and survey responses, traced remarkably similar patterns [3] . This was a correlation between averages for the seven days of the week, not a way to judge any one person’s happiness. Yet it showed that emotional behavior can be studied in everyday life without asking people how they feel. Could we compare entire countries through their smiles? There was a catch: those roughly 405,000 recordings came from just 233 volunteers. Comparing countries would require different sources altogether. SOSI: A global smile ranking of 125 countries and territories We assembled data from 24 groups of studies using different methods , examined how comparable they were and constructed 10,740 pairwise comparisons between countries and territories. The material ranged from reactions to advertising and social-media photographs to portraits of politicians and executives, plus observations of service workers and passers-by. In 19 groups, at least two countries or territories could be compared. We kept the weaker evidence but gave it less weight. No source was given zero weight. We used the Bradley–Terry statistical model to bring the different studies into a single ranking. We called it SOSI (Synthetic Observed Smiling Index) , our composite ranking of smiles across 125 countries and territories. We published the calculations and source materials in a report by The Null Institute [4] . The two rankings agreed in places, but diverged sharply overall: the United Kingdom came 6th in our smile index, the United States 8th, Canada 45th, Australia 46th, Finland 114th and Japan 122nd. Egypt ranked first, although its position came from only two source families: archival responses to advertising and selected photographs. Change how much weight each source receives, and countries move up or down. That is why the chart shows not only each country’s position in our ranking, but also the range of positions produced by different source weights. Ranges show sensitivity to study weights, not population confidence intervals for smile frequency. Download PNG Copy image How to read the chart. First place is on the left, 125th on the right. The dot marks a country’s position in our ranking. The bar spans the middle 90% of positions obtained when the studies are weighted differently. This is not a population confidence interval. For Finland, we drew on five groups of studies. In all 160 weighting schemes we tested, Finland never reached the top 30 . View the full table: 125 countries and territories # Country / territory Model score Source families Limitation 1 Egypt 84.99 2 2 Panama 80.25 2 3 El Salvador 75.04 2 4 South Africa 74.56 2 5 Chile 73.92 2 6 United Kingdom 73.89 8 7 Brazil 73.52 4 8 United States 72.80 12 9 Nicaragua 71.44 1 Photos only* 10 Bolivia 68.24 1 Photos only* 11 Costa Rica 67.95 1 Photos only* 12 Argentina 67.82 3 13 Venezuela 66.94 1 Photos only* 14 Guatemala 65.22 1 Photos only* 15 Pakistan 64.48 3 16 North Macedonia 64.03 1 Photos only* 17 Kazakhstan 63.70 1 Photos only* 18 Ecuador 63.23 2 19 Fiji 60.51 1 20 Ireland 60.38 5 21 Luxembourg 60.08 3 22 Moldova 59.58 2 23 Netherlands Antilles 59.49 1 Photos only* 24 Italy 59.48 6 25 Puerto Rico 58.87 2 26 Paraguay 58.56 2 27 Dominican Republic 58.26 1 Photos only* 28 Iceland 57.74 2 29 Lebanon 56.68 1 Photos only* 30 Uruguay 56.68 1 Photos only* 31 Laos 56.59 1 Photos only* 32 Serbia 55.47 2 33 Kyrgyzstan 55.13 1 Photos only* 34 Honduras 55.03 2 35 Ukraine 55.03 1 Photos only* 36 Colombia 54.97 2 37 Albania 54.93 1 Photos only* 38 Malta 54.89 2 39 Angola 54.73 1 Photos only* 40 Turkey 54.63 1 Photos only* 41 Peru 54.47 2 42 Spain 54.46 7 43 Israel 54.34 3 44 Romania 53.07 3 45 Canada 52.90 3 46 Australia 52.75 4 47 Belgium 52.74 4 48 Malaysia 52.48 3 49 Myanmar 52.18 1 Photos only* 50 Ghana 52.08 1 Photos only* 51 Uzbekistan 51.66 1 Photos only* 52 Portugal 51.61 3 53 Aruba 51.35 1 Photos only* 54 Bosnia and Herzegovina 51.24 1 Photos only* 55 Bahamas 50.82 1 Photos only* 56 Armenia 50.72 1 Photos only* 57 Cyprus 50.43 3 58 Russia 50.15 4 59 Trinidad and Tobago 49.98 1 Photos only* 60 Norway 49.87 2 61 Brunei 49.77 1 Photos only* 62 Taiwan 49.54 5 63 Belarus 49.24 1 Photos only* 64 Kenya 48.79 2 65 Tunisia 47.66 1 Photos only* 66 France 47.46 8 67 Netherlands 47.44 4 68 Denmark 47.31 5 69 Vietnam 46.97 2 70 Austria 46.63 4 71 Barbados 46.62 1 Photos only* 72 Azerbaijan 46.51 1 Photos only* 73 Germany 46.50 8 74 Slovenia 46.35 4 75 Iraq 45.99 1 Photos only* 76 Greece 45.54 3 77 Sweden 45.44 6 78 Jamaica 45.37 1 Photos only* 79 Bulgaria 45.20 3 80 Georgia 45.09 2 81 Morocco 45.09 2 82 Slovakia 44.87 2 83 Nepal 44.66 1 Photos only* 84 British Virgin Islands 44.56 1 Photos only* 85 Bangladesh 44.04 2 86 Monaco 43.85 1 Photos only* 87 Czechia 43.82 5 88 Philippines 43.79 2 89 Tanzania 43.55 1 Photos only* 90 Jordan 43.25 1 Photos only* 91 Switzerland 43.19 4 92 Sri Lanka 42.86 1 Photos only* 93 Lithuania 42.84 3 94 Palestine 42.76 1 Photos only* 95 Croatia 41.34 3 96 New Zealand 41.17 2 97 Thailand 41.11 3 98 Montenegro 40.95 1 Photos only* 99 Estonia 40.57 3 100 Singapore 40.23 3 101 Mauritius 40.22 1 Photos only* 102 Andorra 40.13 1 Photos only* 103 Qatar 40.13 1 Photos only* 104 Cambodia 39.17 1 Photos only* 105 Oman 38.99 1 Photos only* 106 United Arab Emirates 38.64 2 107 Macao 38.55 2 108 Nigeria 38.19 2 109 Saudi Arabia 38.13 2 110 Bahrain 37.67 1 Photos only* 111 Iran 37.67 1 Photos only* 112 South Korea 37.14 6 113 Latvia 37.10 3 114 Finland 36.61 5 115 Maldives 36.22 1 Photos only* 116 Vatican City 36.22 1 Photos only* 117 Kuwait 36.15 1 Photos only* 118 Hungary 35.66 3 119 Mexico 35.41 4 120 Hong Kong 34.69 4 121 Poland 28.33 6 122 Japan 26.04 8 123 China 23.92 9 124 India 21.12 4 125 Indonesia 19.25 3 * For rows marked “Photos only”, the international comparison rests on a single family of Instagram material. Scores are transformed model coordinates, not measured percentages of smiling people. Source: The Null Institute research report . Even when people are alone, smiles don’t tell the whole story Even if equally good data existed for every country, another question would remain: does the same frequency of smiles signify the same level of happiness? Perhaps different cultures express emotion in different ways. Talhelm, Oishi and Zhang [5] observed people walking alone on university campuses in the United States and China. In the US, 29.1% of passers-by smiled, compared with just 3% in China: a nearly tenfold difference. FIELD OBSERVATION Does smiling require an audience? Researchers observed people walking alone on university campuses in the US and China. Here is the proportion who smiled: US 29.1% China 3% A nearly tenfold difference, even without a conversation partner. Smiling may reflect cultural habits as much as inner happiness. These are local observations, not national estimates. This observation undermines the simplistic idea that Americans smile only for the benefit of the person they are talking to. But even a perfectly measured smile doesn’t necessarily mean happiness. What a cross-cultural psychologist told us When we contacted Kuba Kryś of the Polish Academy of Sciences, he pointed out that smiling may express happiness, but often serves a different purpose: signalling benign intentions or honesty. Such signals are not equally important in every culture. In a 44-culture study , Kryś and colleagues examined how smiling affects observers’ judgments of intelligence and honesty. A separate study by Rychlowska and colleagues explored different social functions of smiles. Neither study measured the happiness of people in our SOSI dataset. This limits our own ranking: differences in smiling cannot automatically be interpreted as differences in experienced happiness. Kryś permitted us to use his observation but has not reviewed or endorsed our analysis. Even so, different methods sometimes picked up similar differences between countries: responses to advertising and Instagram photographs produced partly comparable patterns. Among 31 countries covered by both sources, Spearman’s correlation was 0.42 (p = 0.020; statistically significant). The two methods may be picking up a common signal. Whether that signal reflects happiness is still an open question. One promising comparison is not enough to establish the reliability of the whole ranking. For 50 countries and territories, we had only one photographic source family suitable for comparison. We made 17 comparisons between independent groups of sources covering the same countries. The median rate at which they agreed about which country ranked higher was only 59.1% , insufficient grounds to call our composite table a reliable international measure of human happiness. Smiles proved a much harder way to judge happiness than we had expected. Yet we remain convinced that the World Happiness Report ranking tells us even less about happiness itself. Where are the world’s fastest runners? A survey has the answer Suppose we want to find the country with the fastest runners. Instead of timing races, we survey people and ask: “How satisfied are you with your running fitness on a scale from zero to ten?” We average the answers, draw up a ranking and publish the World’s Fastest Runners Report . The headlines proclaim: “Country with the world’s fastest runners revealed!” The prime minister of Lilliput proudly declares that his nation has the fastest runners on Earth. Yet we have not measured a single person’s speed. The survey might be flawless. But satisfaction with one’s running fitness is not the same as running speed. The mistake is not in the calculation. It is in what we choose to call the result. The word “happiness” in the World Happiness Report raises much the same question. (If the runners example sounds contrived, consider a real one: the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development ( OECD) notes [6] that people in Japan and South Korea rate their health relatively poorly, despite living longer than people in most OECD countries. Such self-assessments of health are also shaped by cultural norms and the wording of the response scale.) The happiness no one asked about The main ranking in WHR 2026 rests on answers to the “Cantril ladder” question. People imagine their best and worst possible lives and place their current life somewhere between zero and ten. The results of representative surveys are then averaged over three years. The word “happiness” does not appear in the question at all. The official FAQ makes this explicit [7] . ANATOMY OF A HEADLINE How does a life evaluation become “happiness”? STEP 1 · GALLUP QUESTION “On which rung of the ladder from your worst to your best possible life do you stand?” ↓ STEP 2 · MEASUREMENT Average life evaluation over three years. ↓ STEP 3 · PUBLIC HEADLINE “Finland is the happiest country in the world.” When did a rating of life become a measure of happiness? We explored the difference between judging your life and feeling happy in Can a Country Make You Happier? . In our relocation calculator , we also explained why a country’s high ranking, by itself, cannot predict how happy someone will be after moving there. But that raises another question: why did the WHR authors choose the title they did? The WHR authors tackle this question themselves. In Chapter 2 (Box 2.2) [8] , they ask why the report is called the World Happiness Report when its primary focus is how people evaluate their lives, not how happy they feel. Their answer is that happiness has both an emotional and an evaluative meaning, and the latter is more useful for analyzing quality of life. The wording is deliberate. The WHR authors use happiness in its evaluative sense, and there are scientific reasons for doing so. But when readers see the headline “Finland is once again the world’s happiest country”, they may assume that Finns experience joy more often than anyone else. The main WHR ranking does not test that. It compares people’s assessments of their lives. That distinction is easy to lose once the results become headlines. REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE How a scientific ranking becomes a political slogan On 20 March 2025, Finnish Foreign Minister Elina Valtonen posted on X: Elina Valtonen on the World Happiness Report 2025: X post dated 20 March 2025 . Click to enlarge the screenshot. The claim also appeared in official government communications. In an August 2025 speech published by the Finnish Government , Valtonen said that “the last eight Happiness Reports tell Finland’s story,” then pointed to education, healthcare and social support. In another official speech, published on 24 August 2026 , she described Finland as: “A country that will continue to be the happiest in the world.” Elina Valtonen’s official speech, 24 August 2026. Read the transcript on the Finnish Government website . Click the screenshot to enlarge. The statement does not establish whether Valtonen intended to conflate life evaluation with emotional happiness. It shows something more readily verifiable: a ranking based on how people assess their lives is presented in official government communication as a claim about national happiness. The ambiguity begins with the name World Happiness Report , and travels well beyond the research. Money, happiness, and the United Arab Emirates Why does the name of a ranking matter? Because rankings travel far beyond academic publications. Political leaders can point to a high position as evidence that their policies are working. In Finland, the foreign minister cited the ranking both on social media and in official government speeches. In the United Arab Emirates, happiness has gone further: the government has built it into public policy and funded international research on the subject. In December 2021, journalist Mara Hvistendahl reported in The Intercept [9] that the Sustainable Development Solutions Network, associated with WHR editor Jeffrey Sachs, had received millions of dollars from UAE-linked entities for research and international work on happiness and well-being. The investigation cited at least $3 million in funding; Sachs confirmed the funding amount to the journalist. In 2016, the UAE appointed a minister of happiness. In 2017, the portfolio expanded to include well-being. In WHR 2026, the UAE ranks 21st worldwide and first among Arab countries. On 24 March, the state news agency WAM [10] proudly reported the result. But the agency got the explanation wrong. It listed GDP, life expectancy, freedom and other factors as reasons for the UAE’s high position. In reality, WHR uses those factors only to explain differences in outcomes. The ranking itself is calculated from people’s answers about their own lives. DOCUMENTED DISCREPANCY The state news agency cited the wrong measures to explain the ranking In reporting the UAE’s result, WAM listed GDP, life expectancy, freedom, generosity and corruption as factors determining its rank. But those factors do not determine a country’s WHR rank. They help explain differences in life evaluations; the ranking itself is determined by answers to the life-ladder question. The public explanation is demonstrably wrong. That is not evidence that anyone interfered with the calculations. Here the contrast is especially stark: the UAE places 21st in the World Happiness Report 2026, but 106th in our experimental index of observed smiles. For the UAE, our index draws on only two source families, so 106th place should not be taken as a reliable assessment of residents’ happiness. Even so, the difference shows how much the picture changes when “happiness” is defined and measured in another way. The timeline matters: the World Happiness Report began in 2012, whereas most of the UAE-linked donations to SDSN, according to the investigation [9] , came between 2017 and 2021. Of the UAE donations listed, at least $1 million went to the Global Happiness Council, which issued recommendations on public policies for happiness [9] . Its 2019 report was presented at an international government summit in Dubai with the support of the UAE Prime Minister’s Office. The UAE was not merely using happiness to shape its own public image. It was also funding efforts to promote the subject internationally. How reliable is our own ranking? For a closer look at the evidence, the expandable note below explains which data we used, how we analyzed them, and where the method falls short. Research methods and limitations The Null Institute smile index is an original secondary analysis of published research. Its 125 geographical units include places other than sovereign states. Of 24 source families, 19 allowed direct cross-country comparisons. The model uses 10,740 derived pairwise comparisons, which are not 10,740 independent observations of individuals . The calculations were successfully reproduced in a verification check. But we",
