      "id": "74b4f7387cd8904ea40aea0f84aa6f4156d2a24920d7974b48d2808aa6576a19",
      "title": "Surprise, surprise: President Trump suggests the midterms were rigged",
      "url": "https://www.poynter.org/reporting-editing/2026/west-virginia-local-journalism-ethics-accountability-dan-parks",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T22:14:10.246Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.poynter.org/reporting-editing/2026/west-virginia-local-journalism-ethics-accountability-dan-parks/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "In a West Virginia county of 6,540, accountability journalism gets personal - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "Former Washington reporter Dan Parks covers neighbors in rural West Virginia, where holding power accountable can complicate personal relationships",
      "evidenceText": "In a West Virginia county of 6,540, accountability journalism gets personal - Poynter In a West Virginia county of 6,540, accountability journalism gets personal - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Ethics & Trust Reporting & Editing Dan Parks, founder and editor of Country Roads, sits in downtown Davis, West Virginia, where his publication covers local news and holds public officials accountable. (Courtesy of Dan Parks) By: Kelly McBride November 9, 2026 Dan Parks is the founder and editor of Country Roads , a Substack publication that serves Tucker County, West Virginia. A couple of weeks before the midterm elections, he was facing an ethical dilemma. A public records request for the details of a legal settlement had finally been fulfilled. The documents showed that the county had reached a $275,000 settlement in May with a former employee who had sued the county after being fired. Her lawsuit alleged sexual harassment by the president of the county commission and other county employees. The commissioner was running for re-election. The election was three weeks away. This seemed like a classic October surprise, a late-breaking, negative news story that can sway voters to change their votes. Earlier in his career, when Parks was working at Bloomberg and Congressional Quarterly, he’d learned to be cautious about such stories, which can be planted by a trailing opponent as a last-ditch effort to change the outcome. The information in the documents was certainly sensational. The lawsuit alleged that county employees were sharing a nude photo of their coworker that her ex-boyfriend had made available. The suit claimed that the president of the county commission told the woman he saw the photo and then became aggressive toward her. The settlement was finalized in May. When Parks called me in mid-October, he was worried that it was too close to the election to publish the story. I told him that October surprises were really about questionable rumors furnished by the political opposition. This didn’t qualify. Instead, it was an issue of legitimate interest to the taxpayers of Tucker County, which has an annual expense budget of $8 million. He published the story on Oct. 21. Two weeks later, the commissioner lost the election by 61 votes. “When I started this publication, I just wanted to cover a lot of things I saw happening in the community,” Parks told me. “I was sitting around getting breakfast or a beer, and people would be talking about, ‘What’s going on here? What’s going on there?’ There’s absolutely no information about major real estate deals and different things happening in the area.” Tucker County, population 6,540, is three hours from Washington, D.C., and three hours from Pittsburgh. It has skiing and mountain biking, and one stoplight in the whole county. It’s the kind of small community that was transformed by the pandemic. Parks has had a weekend connection to the community for more than two decades. And he’s been a homeowner there since 2014. “There’s like an old guard, people who have been there for generations; their granddaddies were coal miners and they were coal miners or timbermen. And then there’s been this influx of people — which accelerated during the pandemic — of people from cities who are much more liberal,” he said. The two groups are often at odds. The newcomers are often disparaged as “tree-huggers” by those who were born and raised in Tucker County. “When I started this publication, I really wanted to be viewed as credible to both of those groups,” Parks said. He has 2,400 newsletter subscribers, including 500 paid subscribers. His open rate for the last 30 days has averaged 59%. A good post will get 10,000 views. He brings in roughly $29k a year and from that Substack takes 15%. His biggest expense is his one reporter, Mat Cloak, a mid-career journalist. Country Roads reporter Mat Cloak, left, and founder and editor Dan Parks in Canaan Valley in Tucker County, West Virginia. (Courtesy of Dan Parks) Together, they publish three to five stories a week, on no particular schedule, whenever they have a story ready. After working in Washington, D.C., for most of his career, the small community was a big adjustment. “There’s a sensitivity to dealing with people who are not accustomed to fielding difficult questions from the media,” Parks said. “I have started emailing sources, never an entire story, but facts and quotes” before publication. He does this for many of his sources, particularly when their quotes go beyond uncontroversial public statements. “I was afraid when I started this little publication that sources would ask me to kill all sorts of things, but that really hasn’t happened,” he said. “For the most part they’ve been reasonable, and they’ve been very grateful, and they’ve also saved me from making numerous errors.” Alan Tomson is the mayor of Davis, West Virginia, in Tucker County. He told me he noticed the impact of Parks’ reporting shortly after Country Roads debuted in November 2023. “Without Dan’s reporting, you could do things under the radar,” he said. “Not intentionally. But people just didn’t know what was going on. Now, I get so many more questions. It keeps everything out in the light of day.” Covering news in a small community where people are not accustomed to journalists asking tough questions has its own form of accountability. As journalists, Parks and Cloak run into their sources all the time. “I think we’ve taken some heat for being what folks say would be divisive or trying to stir up drama,” Cloak told me. “But really we’re just trying to do what we feel like is our due diligence in covering the important issues in the most factual and objective way we can.” The pressure of that familiarity drives them to take great care with every story. The two journalists talk through every decision. Parks frequently calls up old colleagues to discuss tough choices. He called me with his October surprise dilemma at the suggestion of one of those colleagues. Mayor Tomson said he thought it was important that Parks published the story about the commissioner’s role in the hostile workplace lawsuit before the election. “It may or may not damage the candidate’s opportunity to get elected,” he said. “But it’s better for a person to know that before they pull the lever in the booth on Election Day.” When a professional reporter moves to town and trains his skeptical eye on his own small corner of the world, it changes the entire community in ways that can be a bit uncomfortable, Tomson said, even if it’s ultimately better. “We are small. Everybody knows everybody. That commissioner, most everybody knows him personally. They went to high school with him or they’re his neighbor,” he said. “It’s not just some political figure down at the Capitol. It’s personal for everyone.” This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: accountability journalism , community journalism , Country Roads , Dan Parks , independent journalism , journalism ethics , local elections , local news , Rural journalism , Substack , Tucker County , West Virginia Kelly McBride Kelly McBride is a journalist, consultant and one of the country’s leading voices on media ethics and democracy. She is senior vice president and Chair&hellip; Kelly McBride Opinion | Surprise, surprise: President Trump suggests the midterms were rigged The president had appeared to accept the Democrats’ sweeping victories. His latest comments drew exasperated responses, even from some allies. November 10, 2026 Tom Jones Opinion | Want a newsroom job that doesn’t exist? Here’s how to pitch it. You don’t have to wait for the perfect opening. Here’s how to identify a need, propose a new role and convince newsroom leaders to take a chance. November 10, 2026 Cristina Silva Poynter releases new conflict of interest guidelines for journalism funders The free toolkit helps journalism funders anticipate and manage conflicts of interest while protecting editorial independence and public trust November 9, 2026 Lisa Geiger Opinion | CNN’s John King has seen a lot of elections. This one was different. In a special episode of ‘The Poynter Report Podcast,’ the CNN political correspondent discusses Democrats’ sweeping victories and what comes next November 9, 2026 Tom Jones Dan Parks, founder and editor of Country Roads, sits in downtown Davis, West Virginia, where his publication covers local news and holds public officials accountable. (Courtesy of Dan Parks) In a West Virginia county of 6,540, accountability journalism gets personal Former Washington reporter Dan Parks covers 