      "evidenceText": "Trains are still safe | The European Correspondent The European Correspondent Support the crowdfunding Projects About us You're still safer on a train Last week's horrific rail accident in Spain was the second such accident in the country within the past year. Those two crashes have already killed more passengers than in the deadly year of 2013, when 97 passengers died in train accidents, according to Eurostat . The number of major rail accidents and fatalities has generally declined in Europe since 1980 – despite a small uptick in recent years. Trains remain one of the safest modes of transport. As a train passenger, your risk of dying in a rail accident is one third that of a bus or coach passenger and very similar to that of a commercial aircraft passenger. By comparison, car occupants are around 40 times more likely to die than train passengers travelling the same distance. Sebastian Gräff , Laura Bejder Jensen 5 November 2026 at 02:00 Want Europe in your pocket? The best of our journalism. Made to fit your pocket or purse. Once a month in your letterbox. 424 0 PocketPaper subscriptions pre-ordered so far Pre-orders open until 30 November 2026 To launch the PocketPaper, we need 3,000 subscribers. Will you be one of them? 12 editions for €120 a year. You'll only be charged for the PocketPaper if we reach our goal. Pre-order now Europe, visualised Understand how Europe is changing in graphs, charts, and maps. Incumbents lose elections Ever dreamt of living in a castle? Trains are still safe Rioting for a reason European Union What's up in Brussels? Frontex is eyeing sweeping new powers I See You, ICC EU governments are sweating over trade with China Spare some change for the EU? Our best stories Our best, timeless journalism The MEP's guide to milking the European Parliament MEPs face little chance of punishment if they want to squeeze as much as they can from a generous system. Want to understand Europe? Hit the road For the past six decade